  "notes": "Composed of {愛|あい} (love) + {撫|ぶ} (to stroke). Refers to touching with tenderness and affection. Used in both literal and figurative contexts.\n\nUSAGE:\n- {愛撫|あいぶ}する (verb): to caress, to fondle\n- Often appears in literary or formal writing\n- Can describe romantic/intimate touching, maternal affection, or figurative use (wind, sunlight \"caressing\")\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {優|やさ}しく{愛撫|あいぶ}する: to caress gently\n- {愛撫|あいぶ}を{受|う}ける: to receive a caress\n- {愛撫|あいぶ}するように: as if caressing (figurative)\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {撫|な}でる: to stroke, to pet — more casual and everyday\n- さする: to rub gently — focuses on the rubbing motion rather than affection\n- {愛|いと}おしむ: to cherish, to hold dear — emotional rather than physical",
